{
  "teamId": 111,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Boston Red Sox entered the season as defending World Series champions, but the weight of expectation proved heavier than the championship banner. A rocky March gave way to an April that mixed flashes of brilliance — Mookie Betts launching a two-run double in the early days, Mitch Moreland delivering a clutch line drive to center — with the first warning signs from a bullpen that would haunt the team all year. Ryan Brasier, the previous October's hero, became a liability almost immediately, surrendering grand slams and multi-run home runs in the season's opening weeks.\n\nMay and June told the story of a team with an explosive offense barely masking a fragile pitching staff. Michael Chavis announced himself as a legitimate power threat, and Mookie Betts kept providing the signature moments Boston needed. But for every Benintendi homer or Bogaerts clutch single, the bullpen found a new way to give it back. Matt Barnes, Brandon Workman, and Marcus Walden all had their nightmare moments, and a brutal June 26 game against Chicago — where Barnes surrendered a two-run blast from Jose Abreu that swung the game by nearly 0.66 win probability — captured the team's infuriating cycle of offense and squander.\n\nJuly was the team's best stretch, posting a 15-10 record and generating genuine hope that the offense, paced by Chavis and Marco Hernandez stepping into unlikely hero roles on Independence Day, could carry Boston back to October. August brought more of the same tug-of-war: Bogaerts and Bradley Jr. providing thrills while the pitching staff continued to hemorrhage runs, including a brutal late-August loss to the Angels when Albert Pujols singled home two runners to blow a lead. The team stayed competitive but never separated from the pack.\n\nSeptember extinguished whatever playoff hopes remained. The Red Sox went just 11-15 in the final month, with the bullpen delivering gut-punch losses right to the final week — including a Nate Lowe two-run homer off Josh Smith and a Danny Santana grand slam in the penultimate series that encapsulated everything that went wrong. A team that scored 901 runs and featured one of baseball's most dangerous lineups finished 84-78, missing the playoffs entirely. The 2019 Red Sox were a championship hangover made manifest: too good to be bad, too flawed to be great.",
  "chapters": [
    {
